Description: Raise MEMP_NUM_NETBUF to 64 for the sequential (netconn) API

The lwIP default MEMP_NUM_NETBUF is 2. The socket receive path
(recv_udp in api_msg.c) allocates one struct netbuf per received
datagram and holds it until the application calls recv(). When the
socket mailbox holds one netbuf and recv() is copying another, the
next received datagram finds no free netbuf and lwIP drops it without
an error. This caused silent UDP datagram loss on the gVNIC path
(Ticket 9374183aab): the MEMP_NETBUF allocation error count matched
the lost packet count exactly on both peers.

Size the pool like MEMP_NUM_NETCONN. A struct netbuf is small (about
40 bytes), so 64 entries cost about 2.5 KB.

--- a/include/lwipopts.h
+++ b/include/lwipopts.h
@@ -366,7 +366,17 @@
 #define MEMP_NUM_NETCONN CONFIG_LWIP_NUM_TCPCON
 #else
 #define MEMP_NUM_NETCONN 64
 #endif /* LWIP_TCP */
+/*
+ * Number of struct netbuf entries. The sequential API allocates one
+ * netbuf per received datagram. The netbuf stays allocated until the
+ * application reads the datagram with recv(). The lwIP default of 2 is
+ * too small: when the socket mailbox holds one netbuf and recv() is
+ * copying another, the next received datagram cannot allocate a netbuf
+ * and lwIP drops it without an error. Keep the count aligned with
+ * MEMP_NUM_NETCONN.
+ */
+#define MEMP_NUM_NETBUF 64
 #else
 #define LWIP_NETCONN 0
 #endif
